Seraphim wrote on 10 Nov 2003 21:20:50 GMT:

> Durring an eclipse however your pupils don't contract, so all that
> UV radiation (which retains more of its intensity during an eclipse
> than visible light does) will still be frying the back of your eye,
> and there will be a lot more of it than "normal".

Do you mean that while it's useless to put on the dark protective
glasses during the _total_ eclipse part, one should better look
through 100%-UV-filtering glass? I think I have heard a similar thing
somewhere, but can't really see why this should be so.

Sure enough, the moon filters both UV and visible light. So, while
it's dangerous to look at a _partially_ covered sun[1], I can't quite
believe this holds for a total eclipse. I even know many people who
have watched a _partial_ eclipse without proper (some without any!)
protection (couldn't afford it) without further problems. (Not that I
would recommend it.)

I think it's more that eclipse-glasses producers don't especially like
the facts (that the really spectacular part doesn't require anything
they sell) and try to spread some "recent research". Some scientific
studies (referenced in the page below) even show that (*some kinds*
of) compact discs and other everyday objects offer adequate protection
(contrary to popular belief, and to the popular belief that there is a
popular belief to the contrary).

---
[1] http://sunearth.gsfc.nasa.gov/eclipse/SEhelp/safety2.html - a very
good read (with references) if you are interested in the subject.

